Title: The Comped Table
Author: ClearSlate Golden Corpus
Draft date: August 3, 2026

INT. ONDINE - DINING ROOM - NIGHT

Candlelight. White linen. Forty tables of people pretending
they can afford this. Steel letters glow above the brass front doors, visible through the glass:

ONDINE -- TASTING MENU NIGHTLY, RESERVATIONS ONLY

GARETH PEMBERTON, fifty, tan in November, glides the floor
like he owns the weather. He owns Ondine, and would like everyone to know it.

GARETH
Otis. A thumbprint. On my glass. In my restaurant.

OTIS FENWICK, twenty, terrified, nods four times too many.

OTIS
Sorry, Chef.

GARETH
I'm not a chef. I fired the chef. I am a
visionary with a corkscrew.

He drifts to a four-top and turns into pure warmth.

GARETH (CONT'D)
Welcome back. The scallops swam in from Hokkaido this morning.

They did not. They arrived Tuesday in a Sysco truck out of Fresno. Nobody at table six needs to know that.

MARIA SANTOS, thirties, sharp-eyed, pours water with the calm
of someone who has mentally quit four times tonight.

At the pass, BUNNY CHOI, twenty-nine, the sous chef, plates a
dish so precisely it looks like she's defusing it.

BUNNY
(to Otis, low)
Third comped table this week.

OTIS
The math already noticed.

A bottle of Fiji Water sweats on table six, untouched.

GARETH
(to Maria, sotto)
Olive Garden's running "when you're here,
you're family" again. As if anyone's
grandmother deep-fries a breadstick.

MARIA
I'll alert the health department.

GARETH
Don't be cute. Table twelve is late.

She goes. He straightens his cuffs and admires himself in a spoon.

INT. DELIA'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

A studio above a laundromat. Takeout containers. A corkboard of yellowed clippings, one pinned dead center:

CRITIC UNMASKED: VOSS EXPOSED AS FRAUD
THE BAY CITY REGISTER

DELIA VOSS, forties, once the most feared byline in Bay Area
dining, sits on the floor in yesterday's clothes, a career packed into cardboard boxes around her.

She pours wine into a mug that reads WORLD'S OKAYEST CRITIC.

DELIA
(to the mug)
Eleven years of blind tastings, ended by one
maitre d' with a good memory and an Instagram story.

She dials. The screen lights: CALLING MARIA SANTOS, (415) 621-4487.

DELIA (CONT'D)
Maria. It's me. Don't hang up.

MARIA (V.O.)
(filtered)
I'm walking. Gareth docks tips for phones on the floor.

DELIA
I need a favor. The kind that ends careers.

MARIA (V.O.)
Mine or yours? Yours already ended. I watched it happen from table nine.

DELIA
Rude. Accurate. Still rude.

DELIA (CONT'D)
You remember what I was. I got a table at
the French Laundry on four days' notice
because Thomas Keller's people liked my column.

MARIA (V.O.)
And now you're drinking Malbec from a mug at eleven p.m. Growth.

DELIA
Gareth Pemberton built an empire on my
disgrace, and he's still serving Sysco
scallops to people who mortgaged a kidney for the tasting menu. I need eyes inside.

MARIA (V.O.)
There's a comped table Thursday. He comps
one every service for the free post. Get on that list under a name he doesn't know --

DELIA
I could watch him serve truck food and
photograph every plate.

MARIA (V.O.)
If he makes you, he calls security before
you've unfolded your napkin.

DELIA
Then I won't get made.

She catches her reflection in the dark window: hair a wreck,
mascara from Tuesday.

DELIA (CONT'D)
(to the reflection)
Even the rat in Ratatouille had a better
glow-up arc than this.

INT. FERRY BUILDING - DAY

Sun through the clock tower windows. Tourists photograph crab
stands. DELIA finds JASPER COYNE, thirty-eight, forearms scarred from a decade of hot pans.

JASPER
You look like a woman about to ask me for something felonious.

DELIA
Just trespassing. Maybe fraud, if it's a slow week for the DA.

JASPER
Gareth fired me for correcting his knife
grip on camera. I've been waiting eighteen months for this call.

DELIA
I need a face he's never seen and hands that know a real scallop from a frozen one.

JASPER
(raising a cup)
Julia Child never apologized for using too much butter. I'm not apologizing for this.

DELIA
That's not really what that quote's about.

JASPER
Close enough for the Ferry Building.

He slides a napkin across the table, a rough floor plan of Ondine's kitchen drawn from memory.

JASPER (CONT'D)
Service door. Walk-in. The pass, where he
stages the comped table's plates himself.
That's where the Sysco boxes land before they're dressed up.

DELIA
Gordon Ramsay would set that kitchen on fire and call it mercy.

JASPER
Ramsay never survived a Tuesday under Gareth Pemberton. Nobody's soul is that clean.

MARIA arrives, off shift, sliding into the booth with a third coffee she didn't order.

MARIA
You're late.

DELIA
1 Ferry Building has more foot traffic than my whole apartment building. Sue me.

MARIA
Gareth doesn't sue. Gareth talks to a
reporter he keeps on retainer. Ask me how I know.

MARIA (CONT'D)
Reservation's under Diane Voss -- close
enough you'll answer to it, far enough his system won't flag it. Comp table's eight
p.m. Thursday. He does the reveal himself, narrating every plate like a museum docent.
That's your window to shoot the boxes before Bunny breaks them down.

Delia looks past them, out toward the water, where fog still clings to the Golden Gate Bridge.

DELIA
He took my name and made it a punchline.
I'd like it back before I'm too old to enjoy the apology.

She lifts her coffee like a toast. They clink paper cups against it, unconvinced but in.

INT. ONDINE - KITCHEN - NIGHT

Steam and shouting, the controlled chaos of Thursday rush.
OTIS hauls a delivery box through the service door, breathing hard. Stenciled on it: SYSCO.

BUNNY
Break it down before he's back. Put a real
crate in front of it.

Otis shoves the box behind milk crates as GARETH sweeps in,
jacket immaculate, showman fully switched on.

GARETH
Comp table's a Diane somebody. Never heard of
her -- either way, we perform.

BUNNY
Course one's ready, Chef.

GARETH
I'm not a chef.

BUNNY
(under her breath, to Otis)
He says that like we'll ever forget.

Gareth lifts the plate himself -- seared scallops, bright
green puree, gold leaf pricier than the scallop beneath it.

GARETH
Hokkaido. Every time. Say it with me.

OTIS
Hokkaido.

He carries the plate out through the swinging door like a man walking onstage.

INT. ONDINE - DINING ROOM - NIGHT

DELIA, hair pinned, glasses she doesn't need, sits at the
comped table calm as a woman defusing a bomb with a fork. Her phone rests face-down by the bread plate.

GARETH arrives, plate held high, already narrating.

GARETH
Diane. Welcome to Ondine. We open with scallop, Hokkaido, flown in this morning --

DELIA
(pleasant, deadly)
Funny. I could swear I saw a Sysco box go out your service door two minutes ago.

Gareth's smile doesn't move. His eyes do.

GARETH
We recycle our own crates. Cost control. Very of-the-moment.

DELIA
Sure. And the Chateau Larkspur on table four
is a real label, at least. Credit where it's due.

She turns her phone over. Three photos, the SYSCO stencil unmistakable in each.

GARETH
(quiet)
Who are you.

DELIA
Diane Voss. Almost my real name. You unmasked
the last one on Instagram, so I'm returning the favor with better lighting.

The room has gone quiet. Nearby tables are filming now.

GARETH
Delia.

DELIA
There it is. Took you a whole appetizer.

MARIA, across the room, has stopped pretending to work.

GARETH
(low, furious)
You will not do this here.

DELIA
I already did. Three photographs and a
reasonably priced Fiji Water while I waited for you to lie to my face on camera.

He reaches for her phone. She lifts it out of range like she's been doing this her whole career, because she has.

GARETH
Get out of my restaurant.

DELIA
Your restaurant is trending for
truck-to-table, not farm-to-table. I didn't write the headline. I just took the picture.

Gareth's composure cracks. He storms toward the door, shouting over his shoulder.

GARETH
Bunny! Otis! We're closed! Comp the whole room, I don't care!

He shoves through the front door into the street.

EXT. ONDINE - NIGHT

Gareth fumbles his keys at a waiting Tesla. OTIS, who
followed him out mostly to watch, reads the plate aloud with real delight.

OTIS
G-R-V-Y-T-R-N. Gravy train. Subtle, Chef.

GARETH
I'm not a chef!

He peels out, nearly clipping the valet stand. Faint
through the propped door, Sinatra's "My Way" plays on over the restaurant speakers,
like nobody told the sound system the show was over.

INT. ONDINE - DINING ROOM - NIGHT

DELIA stands amid the wreckage of an evening. MARIA crosses to her.

MARIA
You couldn't have just emailed the photos to
a health inspector like a normal person?

DELIA
Where's the theater in that.

BUNNY emerges from the kitchen, apron off, grinning for the first time all night.

BUNNY
For what it's worth, the scallops were fine. Calling them Hokkaido was the crime.

JASPER slides in from the door, having watched the finale
from the sidewalk.

JASPER
He'll sue.

DELIA
Let him. Discovery's going to be a wonderful
week for everybody.

INT. DELIA'S APARTMENT - DAY

Weeks later. The boxes are gone. A new front page holds the
corkboard's center, pinned where the old one used to live:

VOSS VINDICATED: ONDINE OWNER FACES FRAUD INQUIRY
THE BAY CITY REGISTER

DELIA, hair actually brushed, taps an email into her
laptop. The screen reads: FROM delia.voss.eats@gmail.com.

Her phone buzzes. A text from Maria lights the screen:

BUNNY AND I ARE BUYING THE LEASE. REOPENING AS
TWO SPOONS. NEW NUMBER: (415) 555-0142.
FIRST TABLE'S YOURS.

DELIA
(reading, smiling)
Two Spoons.

She saves the contact and closes the laptop, and for the
first time in the whole story looks like she isn't bracing
for impact.

DELIA (CONT'D)
(to herself)
St. Agnes Memorial Hospital gave me a two-week
stay and a pamphlet about stress. This is cheaper,
and it comes with dessert.

She grabs her coat, pauses at the corkboard, and presses one
more pin into the headline to make sure it holds.

DELIA (CONT'D)
Table for one. Uncomped, for once.

She heads out, leaving 1147 Belmont Row behind her, the
door clicking shut on the emptiest the apartment has
looked in a decade.

FADE OUT.
